        {
          "path": "_m/tpc/gr/ea/t-/sa/great-sand-dunes-national-park/small-flowered-sand-verbena-gr.jpg",
          "filename": "Small-Flowered Sand Verbena, Great Sand Dunes.jpg",
          "caption": "NPS/Patrick Myers\nThis rare plant generates many visitor questions each summer. Visitors assume the large, lush seedpod is a blossom, but the flowers are actually the tiny white blossoms at the end of succulent, pink tubes. \nThis verbena looks like it belongs more in a tropical rainforest than a desert - just another hidden surprise at Great Sand Dunes!\n\nThe scientific name for this species is Tripterocalyx micranthus. Because this plant is relatively rare, its historical medicinal uses are not well-known. Regional tribes used related Tripterocalyx species as a diaphoretic, sedative, snake bite treatment, and sore throat treatment.",
          "alt": "NPS/Patrick Myers\nThis rare plant generates many visitor questions each summer. Visitors assume the large, lush seedpod is a blossom, but the flowers are actually the tiny white blossoms at the end of succulent, pink tubes. \nThis verbena looks like it belongs more in a tropical rainforest than a desert - just another hidden surprise at Great Sand Dunes!\n\nThe scientific name for this species is Tripterocalyx micranthus. Because this plant is relatively rare, its historical medicinal uses are not well-known. Regional tribes used related Tripterocalyx species as a diaphoretic, sedative, snake bite treatment, and sore throat treatment.",
          "width": 4608,
          "height": 3456,
          "mimeType": "image/jpeg",
          "license": "CC BY 2.0",
          "artist": "Great Sand Dunes National Park and Preserve",
          "sourceUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Small-Flowered_Sand_Verbena,_Great_Sand_Dunes.jpg",
          "sourceType": "sibling",
          "sourceArticleId": "great-sand-dunes-national-park",
